# P02 adversarial risk register

| Attack | Assessment and required response |
|---|---|
| BV is a routine polynomial-method corollary. | Its proof architecture is standard. Establish whether the exact characteristic-two statement is already known; claim only a properly scoped specialization/synthesis if that is all that survives. |
| A finite-field algorithm solves Deutsch in one query. | Compare oracle and readout contracts. Never dismiss the source or announce a contradiction before checking characteristic and accessible data. |
| G0=G1 invalidates your exact upper bounds. | Correct for broad wording; C17 repairs it. Use QXOR for matching2/n upper bounds and retain arbitrary G only for lower bounds. |
| Adaptivity destroys the degree argument. | Supply a full finite recorded-branch induction with complete injective instruments and maximum query count. Do not use an informal quantum deferred-measurement analogy. |
| Kickback means Fourier should work. | Show the exact nilpotent determinant/Smith-rank obstruction. Distinguish existing eigenvector kickback from an invertible measurement basis. |
| You have proved no modal query advantages. | False; known UNIQUE-SAT is included as a positive control. The three named tasks do not classify all modal computation. |
| Full-description inspection defeats the bounds. | It changes the access model and can reveal global information outside a complete modal readout. Include the ablation and its cost/model distinction. |
| Simon/Grover are broad impossibility theorems. | They are not. Simon is finite-scoped; Grover only diagnoses the conventional sign/diffusion mechanism. |

The most serious risk is prior art making the named lower bounds a small or known specialization. The paper must remain focused and honest even if that reduces its format to a short note; a standalone article is not an entitlement of having many tests.
